    "*Lean on Me* (1989) was filmed primarily in and around **New Jersey**.\n\nWhile the movie is based on the true story of Eastside High School in Paterson, New Jersey, most of the filming took place at other locations to avoid disrupting the actual school.\n\nThe primary filming locations included:\n\n*   **Passaic High School:** Many of the interior and exterior shots of the school were filmed here (located in Passaic, NJ).\n*   **Paterson, New Jersey:** Various street scenes and neighborhood shots were filmed on location in Paterson to maintain the authentic feel of the city.\n*   **Other New Jersey sites:** Additional scenes were shot in various locations throughout the state.",
    "*Lean on Me* (1989) was filmed primarily in **New Jersey**.\n\nWhile the movie is set in Paterson, New Jersey, most of the actual filming took place at the following locations:\n\n*   **East Orange, New Jersey:** Many of the exterior and interior school scenes were filmed at the **East Orange High School**.\n*   **Paterson, New Jersey:** Some location shots and B-roll were filmed in Paterson to maintain the authenticity of the setting.\n\nThe production used East Orange High School because it provided the necessary space and architecture to represent the fictionalized version of the school in the film.",
    "*Lean on Me* (1989) was filmed primarily in **New Jersey**.\n\nWhile the movie is set at Eastside High School in Paterson, New Jersey, the production used several different locations to achieve the look of the school:\n\n1.  **The Exterior:** Most of the exterior shots of the school were filmed at **Eastside High School** in Paterson, NJ.\n2.  **The Interior:** Because the actual school was in use and too small for a full film crew and equipment, the interior scenes were filmed at **Passaic High School** in Passaic, New Jersey. \n3.  **Additional Sets:** Some interior scenes were also filmed on soundstages to allow for more controlled environments.\n\nThe filmmakers chose Passaic High School for the interiors because its architecture more closely resembled the \"run-down\" look they wanted for the movie's beginning, whereas the actual Eastside High had undergone some renovations."
